---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead title="About · chenj.dev" description="A bit about me and what I'm learning." />
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main>
			<article class="prose">
				<h1>About</h1>
				<hr />

				<p>
					Hi, I'm Jie. I'm a developer on a focused, self-directed journey into DevOps and Platform
					Engineering.
				</p>

				<p>
					For the better part of a year, I'm going deep on the infrastructure side of software — the
					things that happen before and after code is written. How it gets built, deployed, scaled,
					monitored, and kept running.
				</p>

				<h2>What I'm learning</h2>

				<ul>
					<li>
						<strong>Cloud (AWS)</strong> — EC2, S3, IAM, VPC, EKS, RDS, CloudWatch, and the broader
						services ecosystem.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Containers</strong> — Docker fundamentals, multi-stage builds, container registries,
						and Kubernetes for orchestration.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Infrastructure as Code</strong> — Terraform for provisioning, Ansible for configuration
						management.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>CI/CD</strong> — GitHub Actions pipelines, GitOps workflows, and ArgoCD for Kubernetes
						deployments.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Observability</strong> — Prometheus, Grafana, structured logging, and alerting patterns.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Linux & Networking</strong> — The fundamentals that make everything else make sense.
					</li>
				</ul>

				<h2>Why write about it?</h2>

				<p>
					Writing forces clarity. If I can explain it, I understand it. If I struggle to write about
					something, it's a signal I haven't fully grasped it yet.
				</p>

				<p>
					This blog isn't meant to be authoritative. It's a learning log — expect rough edges, honest
					confusion, and (hopefully) the moment things click.
				</p>

				<h2>Background</h2>

				<p>
					I come from a software development background, which means I'm not starting from zero — but
					the ops side of the stack is genuinely new territory. That beginner's perspective is part
					of what makes this worth writing down.
				</p>
			</article>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>
